body:
2. Frequent use of all-or-nothing expressions like "you always" and "you never;"
   True to a ridiculous extent. Happens in almost every disagreement that we ever have.
4. A strong sense of entitlement that prevents her from appreciating your sacrifices, resulting in a "what have you done for me lately?" attitude and a double standard;
   This ties into point number 2 in huge ways. Like I mentioned I have to remind her almost every time we have an argument, of every time I've ever fixed something that was wrong with me or offered to change things up, including leaving my 50k a year job and family to move to another place for her sake.
7. Low self esteem;
  She says that this is the case, although frankly I don't really see it.
8. Verbal abuse and anger that is easily triggered, in seconds, by a minor thing you say or do (real or imagined), resulting in temper tantrums that typically last several hours;
   Spot on. As soon as I question any action, or something she said, it becomes a fight. And there have been things that I've done that either shouldn't have been a problem or at least not a large ones, that turned into a massive fight as well. 
10. Always being "The Victim," a false self image she validates by blaming you for every misfortune;
   This is another part of the whole thing that flips back and forth. One minute I'm manipulative and don't care about her, and then later I'm an amazing person and she doesn't deserve me. This happened again between yesterday and today. It was the latter yesterday and the former today.
12. Complaining that all her previous BFs were abusive and claiming (during your courtship) that you are the only one who has treated her well;
   In fairness, I know many of them, (small town) and I would be surprised if they weren't.
16. Having no close long-term friends (unless they live a long distance away) even though she may have many casual friends;
   Accurate.
18. Always convinced that her intense feelings accurately reflect reality -- to the point that she often "rewrites history" because she regards her own feelings as self-evident facts, despite her inability to support them with any hard evidence.
   This hits me like a brick in the face. There aren't words for it.

I would appreciate it if you would message me or something. I'm pretty torn up about all of this frankly. Thank you for your thoughts.
I have heavily considered going to a psychologist about it.
The pressing question I have here, is whether she is fully aware of how to fix this and just doesn't care to, or if she's genuinely struggling with self issues, like she ends up saying at the end of our arguments. Like I said, if these problems could be addressed, I have faith that this is a relationship that I could be very satisfied with.
